m segment of a rib had been removed</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="10-oa-0220f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-10-oa-0220" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>A: Massive parapneumonic empyema in the right hemithorax. Although adequately drained, the lung did not re-expand and there was some remaining air-fluid. B: CT-scan four months after POP insertion. The lung has been completely re-expanded. POP was removed at this occasion. C: Chest X-Ray one month after POP removal</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="10-oa-0220f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-10-oa-0220" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>On the left, a conventional open pleural window scar in a male patient. On the right, the upper scar is after POP removal, and below is a scar after removal of a regular 36<italic><sup>FR</sup></italic> chest tube</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="10-oa-0220f4"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>